with almost 500 sessions at next 2019  it's understandable that you couldn't  get to them all so if you didn't see  best practices GCP resource organization  and access management then stay tuned  because here's the rewind regardless if  you're new to Google cloud platform or a  longtime user it's important to keep all  your GCP resources organized we don't  just mean resources like VMs but  actually at the project and organization  level in this talk max and Greg go over  what these different resources are and  best practices for organizing them it's  important to consider the setup since  almost a third of billing support cases  that we handle end up being resource  management related if you're not sure  how your resources are configured you  may find yourself dealing with confusing  invoices access issues or even potential  disruption of service the best way to  avoid these issues is to make sure  everything is set up to match your needs  let's take a look at the resource  hierarchy here's an overview of the  different resources will step into each  area and talk about different things to  consider your domain and organization  are like an umbrella for everything in  the cloud the domain is done through G  suite or cloud identity and helps you  manage users organizations let you  manage all the GCP resources through the  console together these make sure you  have a way to manage everything from a  high level especially helpful for  situations when someone may have lost  access to a low level resource like a  project you'll have a super admin for  the domain and an organizational admin  for your organization which could be the  same person make sure to consider who  should be the admin in each case such as  the orga demin being a more senior  technical member in charge of all the  cloud projects it's also key to make  sure everyone knows who the admins for  these are and to keep all the roles up  to date try to aim for a reasonable  level of redundancy to avoid any single  points of failure bailing accounts are  under the organization and control how  you pay for anything the associated  projects use they have their own set of  roles and permissions such as the  billing account users who can see how  much each project is spending and Link  projects to a billing account only a  billing account admin can remove  projects from the billing account and  set budgets again it's important to know  who your billing admin is because  they're needed if you ever want to  contact billing support in most cases  you'll only need one billing account per  organization which will also help keep  things simple when looking at what  you're paying per project don't forget  to enable  big query export as soon as possible so  you can further analyze what you're  paying for now the payments profile  actually sits outside the cloud and is  used for paying anything in Google such  as chrome licenses you may want to have  the same admin for both your payments  profile and your billing account but you  can customize who has access to edit  information or only have read-only  access just like your billing account we  typically recommend only having one  payment profile making sure the right  people have access and getting  notifications can go a long way and  avoiding any fire drills you should also  add a secondary payment method and  review the rules here on a regular basis  to avoid any potential problems anything  done on GCP will be done from a project  which are basically like groupings of  direct cloud resources such as  kubernetes clusters these projects can  exist under folders as well as be  labeled so you have full customization  over your hierarchy in fact we recommend  trying to match your company's  environment so maybe you'd have a folder  for each team or different folders for  dev and prod you should use naming  conventions that are familiar to your  team for projects and labels so everyone  knows what each project is for labels  can help group those together each  project also has its own role management  so you can ensure only trusted people  can edit resources inside of a project  or even create new projects this is a  quick overview of the resource hierarchy  but there's plenty more specifics and  best practices to check out which you  can read through in the guide and  checklist linked here max and Greg go  into a deeper dive for each area during  the talk cover more best practices and  walk through an example of what a  company might consider for the resources  as they grow if you'd like to see the  full talk check out the link in the  description below and don't forget to  subscribe to the cloud YouTube channel  for more great next rewind content  thanks for watching  [Music]
 

